# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Root environment for deploying Beaconfolio under YOUR own name and domain.
#
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and adjust. Every value below has a safe default
# baked into the compose files / proxy, so an unset variable keeps the canonical
# behavior — a forker only needs to override the handful that identify THEM.
# Docker Compose auto-loads `.env` from the project root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Container images (registry / org / name) ------------------------------
# Prod compose pulls `${IMAGE_REPO}-{backend,frontend,admin-frontend,proxy}:${IMAGE_TAG}`.
# Prod compose defaults to `ghcr.io/mavrovde/beaconfolio` (what CI publishes); the dev
# compose uses a local `mavrovde` prefix. Point IMAGE_REPO at YOUR registry/org to
# pull/deploy your own images:
#   IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/<your-gh-user>/<your-repo>
# IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/mavrovde/beaconfolio
# Pinned release tag to run in prod (defaults to the repo's current release —
# see the `VERSION` file; also published: `sha-<gitsha>` and `latest`).
# Deliberately a placeholder, not a concrete version: this file is not a version
# carrier, so a real number here just goes stale every release (it sat at 1.9.0
# into the 1.10.0 release) while `bump_version.sh --check` and the CI
# version-consistency gate look right past it.
# IMAGE_TAG=X.Y.Z
#
# NOTE: CI publishing (.github/workflows/deploy.yml) is driven by GitHub *repository
# variables*, not this file — set REGISTRY and IMAGE_NAME under
# Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables to retarget the publish.
# Defaults there keep publishing to ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>.

# --- Multi-project host: being a good tenant (#310) --------------------------
# Full runbook: docs/wiki/production-deployment.md (host lifecycle, shared edge,
# TLS, port registry). This block is only the knobs that live in THIS file.
#
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME — the prefix on every container, network and VOLUME name.
# Unset, Compose derives it from the deploy DIRECTORY BASENAME, which makes your
# data addressable by an accident of pathing: a directory named `example.com`
# normalizes to `examplecom`, and the volume destroyed in the #91 incident
# carried exactly such a directory-derived prefix. Rename or move that directory
# and every volume is orphaned — an empty database that looks like data loss.
#
# ⚠️ CONTINUITY RULE, same shape as the #288 POSTGRES_DB pin: setting this on an
# EXISTING deployment re-points the stack at NEW, EMPTY volumes unless the value
# matches the name already in use. Read the current name off the host FIRST:
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml ps --format '{{.Project}}' | head -1
#     docker volume ls --filter name=postgres_data     # -> <project>_postgres_data
# then pin exactly that, character for character. An EXISTING deployment keeps
# whatever those commands report; a FRESH install should use the product name:
#   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=beaconfolio
# Deliberately left UNSET below: a value here would silently orphan the volumes
# of every host whose directory basename differs from it.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=
#
# Published host bindings (prod compose only). Defaults reproduce the pre-#310
# behaviour exactly (proxy on host-global 80 + 10443). On a SHARED host the edge
# owns 80/443 and beaconfolio becomes an internal upstream on loopback high ports
# it registers. The dev stack keeps its own fixed 4200/443 bindings and ignores
# these — a developer machine has no neighbour to collide with.
# PROXY_HTTP_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:18080:80
# PROXY_HTTPS_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:18443:443
#
# Postgres host bind (prod compose only; the dev stack keeps "5433:5433" so
# pytest and psql reach it as before). Default 127.0.0.1 — do NOT "fix" exposure
# with ufw: Docker publishes ports via a PREROUTING DNAT rule and the packet
# never enters the INPUT chain ufw filters, so `ufw deny 5433` does not close a
# published port.
# A loopback bind does, unconditionally. Reach the DB from a workstation with
# `ssh -L 5433:127.0.0.1:5433 deploy@host`, not by widening this.
# POSTGRES_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
#
# Log rotation — the json-file driver is UNBOUNDED by default, so one chatty
# tenant fills the disk for everyone. Applied to every service in both compose
# files; can also be set daemon-wide in /etc/docker/daemon.json.
# DOCKER_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
# DOCKER_LOG_MAX_FILE=3
#
# Memory CEILINGS (prod compose only; a ceiling is not a reservation). They exist
# so a runaway tenant cannot OOM-kill a NEIGHBOURING project. Ollama is the named
# hog: model pulls plus OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=-1 keep models resident indefinitely.
# Size them for YOUR box — see the wiki's per-tenant allocation table.
# OLLAMA_MEM_LIMIT=8g
# BACKEND_MEM_LIMIT=4g
# DB_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# OPEN_WEBUI_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# FRONTEND_MEM_LIMIT=1g
# ADMIN_FRONTEND_MEM_LIMIT=512m
# PROXY_MEM_LIMIT=512m
# MAILER_MEM_LIMIT=256m

# --- Site identity (#65) — rebrand a PREBUILT image with env alone ----------
# Served to the public frontend at runtime via GET /api/app/config/site;
# setup.sh prompts for the first four. Empty values fall back to the app
# defaults (never blank branding); analytics is OFF unless you set an id.
# OWNER_NAME=Jane Doe
# OWNER_HEADLINE=Software Engineer
# SITE_NAME=My Portfolio
# SITE_URL=https://example.com
# OWNER_DESCRIPTION=Professional portfolio of Jane Doe, a Software Engineer.
# SOCIAL_LINKS=https://linkedin.com/in/janedoe,https://github.com/janedoe
# BEACONFOLIO_ANALYTICS_ID=
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4200,https://example.com

# --- AI-agent discoverability (#252) ----------------------------------------
# Recruiter research increasingly runs through AI assistants, so the site is
# built to be READ by them: /llms.txt maps the site for agents,
# /api/app/profile/resume.json serves the profile as JSON Resume, and
# robots.txt names the AI crawlers explicitly. Default `allow` welcomes them;
# set `deny` and ONLY the AI user-agents get `Disallow: /` (classic search
# engines are untouched). Anything unrecognized is read as `allow`.
# AI_CRAWLER_POLICY=allow

# --- Public domain / proxy server_name -------------------------------------
# The reverse proxy answers on these hostnames (space-separated is allowed).
# Set them to YOUR domain; loopback + localhost stay allowed automatically.
# UNLIKE the other knobs here, DO set these two: the compose fallback still names
# the canonical instance's hostnames, so leaving them unset serves your site under
# someone else's domain name (#313 — the fallback itself is unchanged on purpose,
# so no existing deployment is repointed).
# PUBLIC_SERVER_NAME=example.com www.example.com
# ADMIN_SERVER_NAME=admin.example.com admin.localhost

# --- Admin console access hardening (SECURITY, #86) ------------------------
# The admin subdomain ships CLOSED to the public. With ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS empty
# (the default) only loopback reaches it; the proxy appends a final `deny all;`,
# so anything not explicitly allowed is rejected. NEVER emits a blanket allow.
#
# HOW IT WORKS: in the containerized prod topology admin traffic reaches nginx via
# the Docker bridge gateway (Docker NAT masks the external source IP), so the proxy
# uses nginx real_ip to recover the true client IP from the front proxy's forwarded
# header BEFORE applying the allowlist. Two knobs must agree:
#
#   1) TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS — upstream CIDR(s) nginx trusts for the forwarded header.
#      Default 172.16.0.0/12 (Docker bridge range). Tighten to your exact front-proxy
#      CIDR if you can. Only trust what you control — a trusted hop can spoof the header.
#      The BACKEND reads the SAME variable (#273) to decide whose X-Real-IP /
#      X-Forwarded-For it believes when keying the per-client-IP rate limiters. It is
#      the address of the hop that talks to the backend — the nginx container in the
#      shipped topology, hence the same Docker-bridge default. EMPTY = trust nothing:
#      every caller is then keyed by its own peer address (correct for a backend
#      exposed directly, wrong behind a proxy — there it collapses all clients into
#      ONE bucket, so set it whenever something proxies to the backend).
#   2) REAL_IP_HEADER — the header carrying the real client IP. Default X-Forwarded-For.
#      If whatever terminates TLS at your edge sends X-Real-IP instead, set that.
#   3) ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS — the real operator IPs/CIDRs allowed in (space/comma list).
#      Use YOUR OWN IPs. The sample below uses RFC 5737 documentation addresses on
#      purpose — pasted as-is they match NO real client, so admin 403s for everyone
#      (that exact mistake happened; the proxy now WARNs at start on 192.0.2.0/24,
#      198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24 and 2001:db8::/32 entries, #336).
#
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.16.0.0/12
# REAL_IP_HEADER=X-Forwarded-For
# ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS=203.0.113.7 198.51.100.0/24
#
# Never set ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS=0.0.0.0/0 in production — that re-opens admin to the
# world.
#
# BREAK-GLASS (measured, #336): loopback THROUGH THE PROXY from the host does NOT
# work once ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS is non-empty — a request to the published port
# (e.g. https://127.0.0.1:18443) reaches nginx as the Docker bridge-gateway IP:
# trusted for the forwarded header but carrying none, so it is matched against the
# allowlist as-is and denied. The paths that DO work when you are locked out:
#   1) Backend-direct (bypasses nginx and the allowlist entirely):
#      `docker compose exec backend curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/...`
#      — enough to verify credentials/health from ON the box.
#   2) True loopback INSIDE the proxy container's network namespace, so nginx sees
#      127.0.0.1 (always allowed): `docker compose exec proxy wget -qO- \
#      --no-check-certificate --header 'Host: admin.<your-domain>' https://127.0.0.1/`
#      (an SSH tunnel works only if it terminates inside that namespace — a plain
#      `ssh -L` to the host lands on the published port and is denied as above).
# The durable fix: add your current IP to ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS and
# `docker compose restart proxy` — the config is regenerated at container start.

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres port (container listen port + host mapping + backend DATABASE_URL).
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# POSTGRES_USER=postgres
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
# Database name. Default is `beaconfolio` since the #330 rebrand (it was
# `hirefolio` from #288, and `mavrov` before that). <!-- de-brand:historical: prior defaults, needed for the pin -->
# ⚠️ UPGRADE NOTE for volumes created under an older default: Postgres reads
# POSTGRES_DB only at volume init, so the data is fine, but the backend would
# look for `beaconfolio` and not find it. Pin the name your volume actually
# holds, explicitly:
#   POSTGRES_DB=hirefolio   # volume initialised between #288 and #330 <!-- de-brand:historical: prior default, needed for the pin -->
#   POSTGRES_DB=mavrov      # volume initialised before #288
# Fresh installs need nothing; they get `beaconfolio`.
# POSTGRES_DB=beaconfolio

# --- App secrets / integrations --------------------------------------------
# Admin password (SECURITY, issue #142). This is the password the backend
# SEEDS and ROTATES the "admin" account to on startup — REQUIRED for a real
# deployment. Behavior:
#   * Fresh DB (no users): the admin is created with this password. If unset,
#     the backend REFUSES to seed a login-able admin (never ships admin/admin).
#   * Existing DB: if the admin is still on the historical weak "admin"
#     password, startup automatically rotates it to this value (closing the
#     legacy admin/admin login). A password an operator already changed via the
#     admin UI is left untouched — this only replaces the known weak default.
# Choose a strong, unique value. Username is "admin".
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=change_me_to_a_strong_password
#
# JWT signing secret (SECURITY, issue #177). REQUIRED for production: this is
# the key that signs and verifies admin bearer tokens. There is deliberately NO
# default any more — the backend REFUSES TO START when it is empty (or set to
# the historical "your-secret-key-change-in-production" placeholder), because a
# publicly-known signing key lets anyone mint an admin token without logging in.
# Generate a strong value with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it invalidates existing admin sessions (one re-login) — which is
# exactly what you want if a weak key was ever deployed.
# JWT_SECRET_KEY=
#
# Local/E2E ONLY escape hatch: with no JWT_SECRET_KEY, generate a RANDOM
# per-process secret instead of failing to start. Tokens stay unforgeable but do
# not survive a backend restart. The dev stack (docker-compose.yml) and the E2E
# overlay enable it; prod ships with it off — never enable it in production.
# JWT_ALLOW_EPHEMERAL_SECRET=false
# BEACONFOLIO_GEMINI_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
# SECURITY (issue #143): Fernet key that encrypts the per-user Gemini API key
# at rest. Empty leaves values plaintext (backward compatible); set a value in
# prod to encrypt the paid credential. Generate with:
#   python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
# Existing plaintext keys are NOT retroactively encrypted when you set this later
# (the encrypt0002 migration ran once) — after enabling, either re-save the key
# via the admin UI or run once:
#   cd backend && BEACONFOLIO_GEMINI_ENCRYPTION_KEY=... python -m scripts.backfill_encrypt_gemini_key
# BEACONFOLIO_GEMINI_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# LINKEDIN_IMPORT_TOKEN=your_machine_token_here
# Directory (inside the backend container) where the saved LinkedIn login
# session is stored. Defaults to /data/linkedin_cookies, which is backed by the
# `linkedin_cookies` named volume so the session survives container recreates.
# NOT settable from this file in the Docker stacks, deliberately: neither compose
# file forwards it, because the path is the TARGET of that named-volume mount —
# changing the variable alone would point the app at an unmounted directory and
# silently stop persisting the session. To relocate it, change the mount target
# in docker-compose.yml + docker-compose.prod.yml. (Bare-metal runs read it from
# backend/.env as normal. Surfaced by scripts/check_compose_env.sh.)
#   LINKEDIN_COOKIES_DIR=/data/linkedin_cookies

# --- Operational tuning (issue #207) ----------------------------------------
# Every value below defaults to the literal it replaced, so leaving this whole
# block commented out reproduces the previous behaviour exactly. Change one only
# when your host tells you to — these are the values whose *correct* setting
# depends on the machine, not on the application.

# Ceiling for a full LLM completion. The slowest thing the backend waits on.
# Raise it on a small host where a cold model takes minutes to answer; lower it
# if you would rather fail fast than hold a worker.
# LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# Budget for a streamed chat POST, where the FIRST chunk (not the whole answer)
# is what has to arrive in time.
# LLM_STREAM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Embedding calls, which are far cheaper than generation.
# EMBEDDING_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Liveness probe against Ollama. Deliberately short: it decides the reported AI
# status and must never hold a request open. Raise it only if a loaded host
# makes the status flap while Ollama is in fact healthy.
# OLLAMA_HEALTHCHECK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=2

# The one-shot startup infra check, which may race a still-booting Ollama and so
# tolerates more than a per-request probe.
# OLLAMA_STARTUP_CHECK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# Multi-agent conversation pre-flight probe; a failure aborts the whole
# conversation, so it is more forgiving than the stats healthcheck.
# OLLAMA_PREFLIGHT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5.0

# Fetching profile JSON from the frontend container.
# PROFILE_DATA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5

# Ceiling for the psql subprocess behind the admin SQL restore. A large dump
# legitimately needs longer than the default.
# DB_RESTORE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# Bulk posts-JSON import guards (siblings of IMPORT_MAX_IMAGE_MB, configured
# in the compose files / backend environment).
# IMPORT_MAX_POSTS_JSON_MB=10
# IMPORT_MAX_POSTS_PER_REQUEST=500

# Per-client-IP budget for the tailored-link public WRITES (#250):
# `POST /for/{slug}/visit` and `GET /for/{slug}/cv`. Both append a row to the
# owner's opportunity timeline and both are unauthenticated by design — the
# slug IS the access control and the link is meant to be forwarded — so this is
# what stops a forwarded URL from growing the database without bound and
# inflating the visit signal the owner acts on. Raise it only if legitimate
# recruiters share one NAT address and hit 429s; the read path (`/for/{slug}`)
# is deliberately NOT limited, because SSR fetches it from the frontend
# container under a single IP.
# TAILORED_VISIT_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=20
# TAILORED_VISIT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# --- Pinned base images (documented; edit in docker-compose.prod.yml
#     if you must move them) --------------------------------------------------
#   db:         pgvector/pgvector:pg16
#   ollama:     ollama/ollama:0.5.7
#   open-webui: ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:v0.11.0

# --- Owner notification channels (#263) — all optional; empty = channel off ---
# Telegram: 2 minutes with @BotFather (see README "Owner notifications").
BEACONFOLIO_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
BEACONFOLIO_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# Provider-agnostic webhook (Slack/Mattermost/ntfy incoming-webhook URL).
BEACONFOLIO_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Per-request timeout for messenger/webhook calls (seconds; #207 knob).
NOTIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# --- Email (#262) — all optional. Dev needs NOTHING: the bundled Mailpit
# catch-all captures every mail at http://localhost:8025. For real delivery
# point these at an external provider (recommended) and set SMTP_STARTTLS=true;
# see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md "Email options" for the self-hosted relay's caveats.
# COMMENTED on purpose (#296 round 3): setup.sh copies this file to .env, and
# an UNCOMMENTED empty SMTP_HOST beats the compose `-` fallback — which
# silently disabled the bundled dev Mailpit for every fresh clone. Uncomment
# only what you actually set.
#SMTP_HOST=
#SMTP_PORT=587
#SMTP_USER=
#SMTP_PASSWORD=
#SMTP_STARTTLS=true
#SMTP_FROM=
# Only for the opt-in `mail` compose profile (self-hosted relay):
#MAIL_SENDER_DOMAIN=

# --- Transparent translation (#248) — on by default, fully local via Ollama ---
# Set false to disable cleanly (no background tasks, no UI remnants).
#TRANSLATION_ENABLED=true
# The language recruiter messages are translated INTO (ISO 639-1).
#OWNER_LANGUAGE=en

# --- Engagement analytics (#249) — first-party, owner-only, no third party ---
# Off means OFF: no event row is written and the admin dashboard's endpoints
# 404, so the screen has no route to reach.
#ENGAGEMENT_ANALYTICS_ENABLED=true
# Retention for engagement events, in days. 0 keeps nothing (purge deletes
# everything up to now); a NEGATIVE value disables purging. Purging deletes
# events only — the CV requests and inbox rows they point at are untouched.
# Apply it from the admin dashboard ("Purge old events").
#ENGAGEMENT_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# How many analytics writes may hold a database connection at once, and how
# many may WAIT for one (#326). Analytics has its OWN small pool of exactly
# ENGAGEMENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_WRITES connections, so it can never take one a
# request is waiting for; an emit arriving with the pending cap already reached
# is dropped, COUNTED and logged (never silently lost). Raise the first only if
# the log shows drops under sustained load — and remember every extra slot is
# an extra Postgres connection.
#ENGAGEMENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_WRITES=4
#ENGAGEMENT_MAX_PENDING_EVENTS=1000

# --- Database connection pool (#326) -----------------------------------------
# The REQUEST pool: DB_POOL_SIZE steady connections plus DB_MAX_OVERFLOW burst,
# i.e. a hard ceiling of 60 with the defaults, comfortably inside Postgres'
# default max_connections=100 for a single backend replica. One in-flight
# request holds exactly ONE of these; anything that needs a second connection
# while holding its first can deadlock the pool against itself (that was #326).
#DB_POOL_SIZE=20
#DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=40
# SQL statement logging. Leave false in production: true logs every statement
# AND every bound parameter of every request (it was hard-coded true until
# #326). Handy for a local debugging session, nowhere else.
#DB_ECHO=false
